00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.189 align:start position:0%
 
okay I want to talk today about  something called the narcissism of small  differences or the narcissism of minor  differences as it's sometimes known and  this would the announcement that Bernie  Sanders is running he's now in the  twenty twenty primary it's a really  important conversation for the left to  have and it's an important conversation  because of some of the factions that are  developing on the left in short terms  and sort of like the briefest way to  define this the narcissism of small  differences is when the small  differences that separate certain people  are overstated to such a degree that  they appear to be massive differences  and cause conflict when there really  doesn't need to be any conflict this  goes back to the 1920s and Freud  actually but there's lots of different  interpretations as to where this  originally comes from but the idea is we  become narcissistic about our own views  on small differences with other people  why this exists is open to some  interpretation there's one idea more  from Freud that there's a kind of  satisfaction to humans of an inclination  to aggression a mild inclination to  aggression and if it seems like we agree  on 95% of stuff of political issues for  example something in us becomes  satisfied when we find something we can  be aggressive about or hostile about  with other people so we focus  disproportionately on the 5% difference  rather than the 95% agreement or  similarity another idea about where the  narcissism of small differences comes  from is that there's just a kind of  general desire to be different and so  when we see that we have a lot in common  with someone else we have a resistance  to feeling that we're just like them so  we will find small differences to argue  about so that we feel unique Christopher  Hitchens had many interesting  commentaries that sort of circled around  this idea the reason that this matters  is that when you become consumed by  minor differences  things start to become exclusionary I'm  separate from those who have this minor  disagreement with me and we start to  focus on the trivial and kind of miss  the forest for the trees to use a  metaphor so let's now look starting with  the 2020 primary at how this is starting  to manifest already it's a primary right  so we've got to make a choice between  the options we do have some duty to  figure out what are the differences  between the candidates and there are  important differences but then there's  also minor differences that are not  practically speaking super-important  let's look for example at Elizabeth  Warren and Bernie Sanders on policy  positions Bernie and Elizabeth Warren  are very very similar in terms of where  they've ended up now I've pointed out to  you Elizabeth Warren comes from the  center more and has been pulled to the  left bernie comes from more of a left  socialism perspective and has been  pulled towards pragmatism he and  Elizabeth Warren have basically ended up  in the same place on most policies not  on all great we don't have to go crazy  arguing about Warren versus Bernie in  the destructive way that I'm already  starting to see friends do on Facebook  they're two of the leftmost candidates  they are arguably the two left most  members of the United States Senate if  either of them won the primary I would  gladly vote for either let's not allow  narcissism over small differences to  cloud that they're not only obviously  better than Donald Trump but they're  mostly better than a lot of the other  Democratic candidates as well in  geopolitics we have lots of examples of  the narcissism of small differences the  partition of India and Pakistan is an  example that maybe is less well known  but a good example let's I mean I'll  take this head-on let's look at an  example that's related to me there are  people on the Left who disagree with me  about the israeli-palestinian conflict  let's explore this like adults for a  second since I know many of you are  probably already thinking about that  how significant are our differences  really in practice I would imagine that  I and most of my audience agrees that  the current israeli-palestinian status  quo is unacceptable  I imagine that we mostly agree that  Benjamin Netanyahu is not going to be an  arbiter of peace in the Middle East I  think we all basically agree that the  Palestinian people are being treated  like crap as a result of what's going on  and we need the most direct path to  ending that that we can possibly find  I would assume my audience and I mostly  agree that the blockade on Gaza has to  be lifted as a top priority of whatever  agreement is made as soon as an  agreement as possible on settlements I  think my audience mostly agrees with me  that new settlements must stop and as  many as reasonably possible are going to  have to be dismantled as part of an  eventual long term peace deal where  might we disagree I've said that I think  there are probably some settlements that  might have to be land swapped in an  eventual agreement maybe some people in  the audience disagree with me about that  but it's a relatively minor difference  my audience and I are I believe on the  same page in opposition of moving the  American Embassy in Israel from Tel Aviv  to Jerusalem so in the end the  differences are relatively minor but  there is a tendency to focus on those  differences overstate them and end up  losing all possibility of sensible  conversation on that basis so what does  this mean for 2020 what does this mean  for the left going forward what this I  think means what I think the takeaway  has to be is let's understand that in  general the narcissism of small  differences is something that can be  destructive let's further our  understanding even in just thinking back  to the interview that I just did that  social media makes this way worse  because it leads to a situation where  oftentimes people are interacting on  social media  only on the basis of the small  disagreements you're not likely to tweet  back and forth with people or to simply  comment on YouTube about how you agree  about ninety-five percent on about 95  percent of issues with someone else  that's on YouTube or on Twitter you're  likely to engage only on those small  differences and that just exacerbates  the problem and the last thing I will  say is that as important as it is not to  fall into the trap of the narcissism of  small differences let's not mistake real  differences as small right so one  example Trump versus Hillary as we are  now seeing with the embarrassment of the  US on the world stage the Supreme Court  decisions that Donald Trump has made and  everything else  some people incorrectly assess the  difference between Trump and Hillary as  small in 2016 that's also a danger we  have to recognize serious differences  where they exist look forward to hearing  from you about this

